#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:11
msgid "Agreement and Good Faith Methodology"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:13
msgid ""
"**Purpose** The agreement_and_good_faith methodology certifies that a "
"contributor has read, understood, and agrees with the content of an "
"assertion based on their technical capabilities and in good faith."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:16
msgid ""
"**Scope** This methodology covers assertion files in the "
"``docs/source/validation/assertions/`` directory, including:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:19
msgid "``.rst`` files (documentation assertions)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:20
msgid "``.md`` files (markdown assertions)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:21
msgid "``.png`` files (visual assertions)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:22
msgid "``.svg`` files (diagram assertions)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:24
msgid "**Protocol Steps**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:26
msgid ""
"**Thorough Reading/Review**: - Completely read the assertion content "
"(text, diagrams, or visual elements) - Ensure understanding of all "
"technical concepts and claims presented"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:29
msgid ""
"**Technical Capability Assessment**: - Evaluate the assertion within the "
"scope of your technical expertise - Identify any areas beyond your "
"current understanding - Seek clarification if any aspect is unclear"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:33
msgid ""
"**Good Faith Agreement**: - Confirm agreement with the assertion content "
"to the best of your understanding - Acknowledge any limitations in your "
"technical knowledge - Document any reservations or qualifications if "
"applicable"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:37
msgid ""
"**Responsibility Acceptance**: - Accept responsibility for the "
"consequences of agreeing with the assertion - Commit to updating your "
"position if new information emerges"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:41
msgid ""
"**Trust Level** This methodology provides **personal certification** - it"
" represents an individual's good-faith agreement based on their current "
"technical understanding, but does not guarantee objective truth or "
"comprehensive verification."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:44
msgid ""
"**Applicable Context** Use this methodology for: - Assertions about "
"software behavior or capabilities - Documentation accuracy claims - "
"Architectural or design principle assertions - Best practice "
"recommendations - Compliance or standard adherence statements"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:52
msgid ""
"**Limitations** - Agreement is subjective and based on individual "
"technical capabilities - Does not replace empirical testing or formal "
"verification - Should be used alongside other validation methodologies "
"for critical assertions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:57
msgid ""
"**Ethical Considerations** Contributors should only use this methodology "
"when they have genuinely: - Made reasonable efforts to understand the "
"assertion - Applied their technical knowledge appropriately - Acted in "
"good faith without conflicts of interest"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:64
msgid "Supported paths"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:66
msgid ""
"What this methodology covers is not a file a program produced. It is an "
"**assertion**: a statement about the software, published as prose, that a"
" person can read, understand and agree with. So its declared path names "
"documentation rather than test output, and a grant under it is a claim "
"about a *statement*, not about any bytes an extraction wrote."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:71
msgid "``docs/source/validation/assertions/**``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:72
msgid ""
"An assertion published in the documentation of **the repository the "
"granted file lives in** -- for the freeports engine, "
"``analysis_finance_reports``. Granting one means the protocol above was "
"applied to that text: it was read completely, judged within the granter's"
" own technical competence, and agreed with in good faith. It covers "
"whatever form the assertion takes there, prose or diagram alike."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:80
msgid ""
"Another project adopting this methodology would keep its assertions "
"somewhere of its own, and this pattern would not reach them. That is "
"deliberate rather than an oversight: a path means something only inside a"
" repository, and the honest way to extend this methodology to a different"
" layout is to say so in a page of your own rather than to widen this "
"pattern until it means nothing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/validation/methodologies/agreement_and_good_faith.rst:86
msgid ""
"This is also why the paths here look nothing like those of ``basic "
"check`` and ``golden standard``. Those methodologies vouch for what a "
"program produced; this one vouches for what a person wrote and another "
"person read."
msgstr ""

